	"EngineVersion": "5.8.0",
	"FriendlyName": "ShroudMap - Fog of War with a Memory",
	"Description": "Fog of war that remembers. Every revealer - unit, tower, vehicle, camera - writes its sight radius into one visibility texture per team, and the map keeps three states instead of two: visible (someone is looking right now), explored (seen before, so the terrain stays drawn but the units inside it do not) and unknown (never seen, fully shrouded). That middle state is what a strategy game actually needs and what line-of-sight products do not keep. Two hundred revealers cost one texture upload per team, not one draw call per revealer, because the whole map is rasterised once and pushed to the GPU in a single region update. Blueprint asks IsVisible, IsExplored and GetVisibilityState in O(1) against a CPU-side mask at the same resolution as the texture - no render target readback, no stall on the game thread. Optional terrain occlusion runs as radial horizon sampling over a coarse height field gathered once at startup, so a ridge casts a real shadow without one trace per cell, and the cost is on the stats box rather than in the small print. Ships with a material function to hook the texture into an existing landscape or postprocess material. ShroudMap does not draw a minimap, does not feed AI perception, does not replicate the fog texture and does not ship units or terrain tools.",
	"Category": "Gameplay",
	"CreatedBy": "Silvan Teufel",
	"CreatedByURL": "https://silvan.teufel-engineering.com",
